Nick Szabo:
> For example, why
> is it that I can remember 100's of tunes, probably several Kbytes
> apiece of data, often after only hearing the tune once or twice, but
> I'm so bad at remembering a few bytes worth of phone numbers?

Tune-memory has several kinds of redundancy, which phone numbers mostly
lack.  (I say "mostly" because I often find patterns that help me remember.
A friend of mine has the last three digits of a famous year in history
followed by two two-digit squares; my father's exchange is his area code
with two digits swapped, and his extension is his employer's initials
followed by the second digit again.)

Many tunes share elements; that's one kind of redundancy (or should I call
it compression?).  You hear the same tune in various contexts and remember
different aspects of it with several parts of your mind; that's another kind
of redundancy.

