From: tcmay@netcom.com (Timothy C. May)
Subject: Mongrelization of America's Intelligence?

Ed Lane writes (in a thread with another name, which I have retitled)

>Tim May asks for documentation:
>
>Heath> >In fact, I would venture further (the not wholly originally idea)
>Heath> >that if we could establish a mean IQ in the year 1950 in the United
>Heath> >States, that the mean IQ in 1993 would be lower. The bell curve is
>Heath> >being shifted down the continuum as the lower educated Americans
>Heath> >continue to have more children than the higher educated Americans.
>Heath> >
>Heath> >-- 
>Heath> >Heath G. Goebel, <capntaz@dudemar.b24a.ingr.com>
>Tim> 
>Tim> Doubtful. Anybody have any data to support this (common) speculation?
>Tim> 
>
>>From the Wall Street Journal, March 15, 1993, "Quantifying America's Decline":
>( sorry they only printed data from 1960 )
>
>Average Daily TV Viewing*       SAT SCORES**
>-------------------------       ------------
>1960    5:06 hours              975
>1965    5:29                    969
>1970    5:56                    948
>1975    6:07                    910
>1980    6:36                    890
>1985    7:07                    906
>1990    6:55                    900
>1992    7:04                    899

Sorry, this just shows that one measure of some kind of intelligence, the
SAT test, has had declining scores. It doesn't say anything about Heath
Goebel's claim that "The bell curve is being shifted down the continuum as
the lower educated Americans continue to have more children than the higher
educated Americans."

There are many issues here:

* from 1960 to 1990, more students took the SAT tests. As the "vocational
ed" track was basically ignored and "universal college education" became
the goal, students who in 1960 would never have taken the SAT test started
to take it. It is reasonable to assume that this would account for some
lowering of the scores. How much is another question.

* ditto for minorities, immigrants, etc. (Again, hard to say what the
effects are. Some groups, like the Asians, have higher than average scores,
while other groups, like the blacks, are far, far below the average.)

* the statistics above say nothing about the sizes of families. 

* during the same time period, gas mileage of cars increased dramatically,
even more so than did hours of television watching. Are we to conclude a
causal relation exists? (I will grant you that I expect more t.v. and less
reading, for example, to be reflected in lower overall scores. But I
haven't seen solid causal evidence.)

In summary, I'm still waiting to see evidence that "overbreeding" by the
stupid is the culprit (if in fact there is a real decline in intelligence,
which I also doubt),

"Tendency to the mean" is, in any case, a powerful factor in this whole
debate. Smart people have children closer to the average, dumb people also
have children closer to the average.

-Tim May, who has posted for the last time, I hope, on this IQ thread.

