Markets aren't "socially constructed."  They're spontaneous orders (like in
the standard explanation of the Extropian principles).  Another way of
putting it is that markets are an emergent property of the interaction of
individuals.  Consider that false claim #1.

Secondly, markets exist over time, too.  The Bank of England has been in
existence since it was founded in 16something by Henry VIII to help finance
his wars.  Over three centuries, and that's just one financial institution.
That's the oldest (semi-) market institution I can think of off the top of
my head, and others will probably be able to best me.  (What is the oldest
market institution?)

Markets contain at least millenia of hard-won information, too.  For example,
it'd be pretty damn hard to come up with a better commodity than gold to
serve as money.  Markets are also rapidly being devoured by statists, too.

The analogy holds pretty well, so far.

>Environmentalists know that the free market/human encroachment is eroding
>habitat, and elimentating diversity.

No, they believe this, but they don't know it.  They aren't capable of
knowing enough about this biosphere to know that.  Is anyone?

>My envoronmentalist friends... have different values. They're not stupid,
>just different. They wont be won over with facile analogies.

It isn't a facile analogy.  Just as there has been conflict between the
supporters of centrally planned economies and their opponents in the past
century, in the next it will be the supporters of centrally planned ecologies
and their opponents.

As for being friends with environmentalists, I've grown up in Berkeley,
where 10% of the electorate registered Green.

>They have data that people on this list aren't
>interested in, about the effect of human activities on ecosytems.

I seriously doubt any environmentalist actually has any data to support their
