Date: Tue, 6 Apr 93 11:04:38 PDT
From: tribble@memex.com (E. Dean Tribble)
Subject: I**2: "innate" intelligence

	 Heh.  If we define intelligence as the amount of time it takes one to tie
	 one's shoelaces, we can dramatically increase our intelligence by buying
	 loafers.

Ironically, this is an extremely important point.  Since joining the
Xanamix/Foresight/Extropian crowd, my heuristics for thinking about
things, solving problems, etc. have all improved, both throught he
example of others and through continued fine-tuning while interacting
with others.  I am definitely smarter (can solve harder problems
faster with less mental effort).  Would I score higher on an IQ test?
I don't know.  It seems to me such development could go for a long
time before we've exhausted teh possibilities.

A fun heuristic: Fredkin's paradox (poorly phrased, I'm afraid): When
choosing between 2 options, if the options are clearly of radically
different value, then the choice is easy.  It's when the options have
approximately the same apparent value that we spend lots of time
wrestling over the choice.  Thje cases where we spend lots of effort
deciding, however precisely those cases in which the difference in value
between one choice and the other is smallest.

Once I heard that, I immediately started recognizing when I was spending
lots of time on a tradeoff, and could quickly decide whether it was
because the tradeoff was so close that in fact it didn't make any
difference.  As a result, many decisions that used to be hard and take
a long time now go very quickly, and I spend my decision capacity on
decisions that are important.

This trivial heuristic continues to make me smarter as I apply it and
generalizations of it to more situations (which car should I buy?
how should I arrange my room? etc.)  

Back to the original point... We gradually embed knowledge and skill
into the artifacts around us (including simple heuristics).  This
makes us more effective, "smarter" by reducing the amount of
processing we need for tasks.  Such a pattern is overall probably much
more effective than merely increasing your intelligence.  Phil Salin
wrote a wonderful paper called "The Nature and Causes of the Wealth of
Kitchens" in which he explores how the knowledge and understanding of
cooking has evolved into the devices and heuristics of cooking, so
that a 'simple' cook effectively manages a complex business without
really needing to understand what's going on.

