} Markets aren't "socially constructed."  They're spontaneous orders (like in
} the standard explanation of the Extropian principles).  Another way of
} putting it is that markets are an emergent property of the interaction of
} individuals.  Consider that false claim #1.

Validation of Claim #1:
Markets are the result of human interaction.  Society is a group
of interacting individuals... so, as an emergent property of the 
interaction of individuals, markets are social constructs.

} Secondly, markets exist over time, too.  The Bank of England has been in
} existence since it was founded in 16something by Henry VIII to help finance
} his wars.  Over three centuries, and that's just one financial institution.
} That's the oldest (semi-) market institution I can think of off the top of
} my head, and others will probably be able to best me.  (What is the oldest
} market institution?)

Validation of Claim #2:
Ed never claimed that markets didn't exist over time.  He did claim
that there was a large difference between the scales of time in 
1) the evolution of the market (social constructs) and
2) the evolution of genetic constructs (biodiversity)
 
The difference between a single millenia and a million millenia   
is rather large.  Developing markets at the cost of the biosphere
is, to environmentalists, a disproportionate cost.  The time required
to redevelop the biosphere is incredibly greater (if not impossible),
and the loss of the knowledge held in the genes is of incredible value.
 
} >Environmentalists know that the free market/human encroachment is eroding
} >habitat, and elimentating diversity.
} 
} No, they believe this, but they don't know it.  They aren't capable of
} knowing enough about this biosphere to know that.  Is anyone?

Validation of Claim #3:
They believe it, and they know it.  No one knows exactly how many
undefined, unrecorded life forms exist in the rain forest... but 
we do know, that thousands of genetic "patents" are being lost 
every year.  It is accepted fact in academic circles, that developing
the economy of countries (especially in the rain forest) comes at 
the cost of reduced bio-diversity.
 
} >My envoronmentalist friends... have different values. They're not stupid,
} >just different. They wont be won over with facile analogies.
} 
} It isn't a facile analogy.

Validation of Claim #4:
The analogy is not too bad.  What Ed is showing, is that there is
a different value system for different people.  For the ultra-extreme
environmentalist, the loss of one animal life... for the benifit of
another bar of soap or another factory, just isn't worth it.

Everyone has different value systems.  What I'm willing to pay for a
car differs from what you would.  What an environmentalist isn't willing
to pay for, is techological advancement at the cost of biodiversity.

} Just as there has been conflict between the
} supporters of centrally planned economies and their opponents in the past
} century, in the next it'll be the supporters of centrally planned ecologies
} and their opponents.

Refutation of Claim A (Tim Starr)
Just because someone values biodiversity over market/economic diversity,
does not mean that they are socialists or central planners.  There are
many non-government free market initiatives that try to protect diversity.
Private purchases of rain forest are one example.

The analogy of environmentalists to central planners does not hold.
There have always been those for and against central planning, but
being an environmentalist doesn't immediately stick you in with the
statists.  (Although there probably is a tendency for the Greens and
granola lovers to work through statist apparati.)

} >They have data that people on this list aren't
} >interested in, about the effect of human activities on ecosytems.
} 
} I seriously doubt any environmentalist actually has data to support their
} statist case that I'm uninterested in.  I strongly suspect that they really
} have little data to support their case.  For example, how many species of
} life are there on Earth right now?  How many have there been in the past?
} At what rate is species extinction occuring?  Verifiable numbers only,
} please.

Clarification of Claim B
Note: I am not an environmentalist... I'm not interested in the 
subject enough to find out all the things that most true granola
lovers know.  Here is some of what I picked up in the old required
gen ed classes... this one, "The History of Life".  I'm separated
from my books, but I can email some friends back home for author
and name if you want.

No one knows exactly how many species are on the planet right now.
Academics have various estimates... ranging between how many of the
existing species we have identified: 2-5%.  Most of the species we
haven't identified are plants and insects... animals from the 
tropical rain forest.

In the past there were fewer species, at one point... none.  The rule
of evolution, is that the fundamental building blocks (Kingdom, Order,
Class) change very little... and that the small adaptations (family and
species) are where most evolution occurs.  So, the true danger of
reducing biodiversity is that you will lose the basic building blocks...
and all the genetic information contained therein.  Of course, Kingdom/
Order/Class what-have-you are only arbitrary catagorizations to describe
the differing levels of diversification amoung lifeforms...  The main
point being there are increasingly less fundamental patterns... and
increasingly more smaller diversifications.  In any case, economic
growth is usually at the cost of both.

I myself could only make vague guess at the rate of extinction...
so I'll refrain from saying anything that I'm not sure I could find
reference to.

Closing:
Myself, I differ from the environmentalists, in that, for me, the
cost of advancing technology is something I'm willing to accept.  I
figure that when technology is sufficiently advanced, we will be 
able to recreate much of what was lost.  And as for the genetic
"patents" that are lost forever... so what?  Technology will eventually
allow us to figure out for ourselves, whatever wonderful combination
of genes was held in an insect that was never known to exist in the
first place.  

Tim S, I guess I just don't want you to think that I'm jumping on
a Enviro-statist stump and preaching the merits of bio-diversity.
I think I must have jumped on the bio-diversity stump... but it 
doesn't necessitate a statist one.

C. Garrett Goebel


