From: Karl Barrus <elee9sf@Menudo.UH.EDU>
Subject: HEX: Reputations and idea futures.

Brian Hawthorne writes:
>possible. Unfortunately, we are currently using a fiat currency with no
>relationship to anything outside the exchange. I'd want to add encryption
>and the like before playing with "real" (or U.S. at least) money.

>2. I would want it to be bullet-proof with authentication/encryption first

Hmm... I have a few questions and comments about this digital cash
system.  I've only been on the list for a week or two, so forgive me
if I missed an earlier explanation.

The way I see the exchange, you start each participant with an amount
of "cash" (maybe everybody starts with the same amout initially; later
people would have different amounts depending on how much "real" money
they put in) which they could trade among each other for goods.  

Is this supervised by your exchange, or could people get the latest
prices (via finger or mail, etc.) and deal on their own and report the
transactions?

Is the bank part of your exchange?  It could conceivably run
separately, with account creation, deposits, withdrawals, etc.
occuring as customers request service.

A simple digital cash protocol would involved random bank account
numbers and random cash.  As long as the bank records the account
number and balance (and doesn't correlate real user id and account
number) this would provide authentication: it would be unfeasible to
guess a correct account number and digital cash if the random strings
were long enough.  A much fancier Chaum-style scheme is also possible.

Encryption would be nice to secure communications with the bank, but
may not be necessary in the beginning, just to get things going.  Or
is it immediately important?

Would it be desirable for the participants to communicate anonymously?
Then two participants could conduct a transaction among themselves
with neither participant knowing the identity of the other, and then
conduct their own banking (possibly with encrypted service requests)
afterwards.

